          %p
            %strong Remark:
            Joyal and Moerdijk actually refine this idea as follows. Instead of taking forests to be set-valued presheaves, they take them to be presheaves over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            valued in a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            equipped with a class of
            %em small maps
            (which are defined axiomatically). Then, a
            %em small forest
            is a diagram in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi …
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi …
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots \to F_{n+1} \to F_n \to \ldots \to F_1 \to F_0
          %p
            where each map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
